/*****************************************************************************
    jitJ2 -- RUNG 2 OF THE jitLadder. 2026-07-31.

        J1 + if/else. Both arms, from ONE compiled function, at run time.

    J1 proved an action compiles, fires, and computes at run time. J2 adds
    exactly one construct: a two-armed conditional. The else arm -- structurally
    absent until 2026-07-31, and the only wrong-answer-at-exit-0 the JIT has ever
    had -- gets its permanent home here.

    ============================================================================
    ⚠ THE TWO FIRES TAKE DIFFERENT ARMS, AND THAT IS STRONGER THAN J1's CRITERION

    J1's two fires proved the *operands* were read at run time. J2's two fires
    prove the *branch* is decided at run time, which is a different claim and the
    one this rung exists for:

        fire 1   jaIn = 10   ->   jaOut = 20     THEN arm
        fire 2   jaIn = -7   ->   jaOut = 7      ELSE arm, NO RECOMPILE

    ONE compiled function, two paths. Nothing between the fires but an
    assignment to jaIn. If both answers are right, the `br i1` was evaluated on
    each call with the live field value -- the condition cannot have been folded,
    because a folded condition would send both fires down the same arm.

    ⚠ AND NOTE WHAT A ONE-ARM RUNG WOULD HAVE PROVED: nothing. The taken case
    was green for a month while the else arm did not exist at all -- correct by
    luck, exercising only the arm that was emitted. That is the same trap the
    two-depth criterion guards against at J-R: A RUNG THAT RUNS ONLY THE EASY
    PATH GOES GREEN ON EXACTLY THE MACHINERY IT EXISTS TO TEST.

    THE VALUES DISCRIMINATE THREE WAYS, deliberately:
        20 / 7   both arms, correct
        20 / 20  the condition was folded; fire 2 took the wrong arm
        20 / 0   the else arm never stored -- the pre-fix bug
    ============================================================================

    SECOND CRITERION: `jitDegrade count = 0` -- no construct in this rung may
    silently fall through to emit-time interpretation.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (standing harness rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitLadder2);
define
    jbIn;
    jbOut;
    /*  J1's assign+arithmetic, now under a two-armed conditional. The else arm
        is a plain literal store so the two arms are visibly different shapes as
        well as different values -- a then-arm that happened to compute the same
        thing as the else would weaken the discriminator.  */
    jitJ2 code={
        if jbIn > 0;
            jbOut = jbIn * 2;
        else
            jbOut = 7;
        };
    ;

jbIn  = 10;
jbOut = 0;
print "J2 fire 1 setup : jbIn =" jbIn " (expect THEN arm)":;
testing(jitJ2);
print "J2 fire 1 result: jbOut =" jbOut " (want 20)":;

/*  ⚠ NO RECOMPILE BELOW THIS LINE. Only the input changes -- and it changes the
    ARM, not just the operand.  */
jbIn  = 0 - 7;
jbOut = 0;
print "J2 fire 2 setup : jbIn =" jbIn " (expect ELSE arm; recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "J2 fire 2 result: jbOut =" jbOut " (want 7; 20 = condition FOLDED; 0 = else arm never stored)":;

/*  ⚠ THE ORACLE'S TESTIMONY, captured LAST and that order is LOAD-BEARING.
    The interpreter is the differential oracle and section 0 sentences it to
    death, so every rung records what it said while it can still say it.
    BUT: calling the action interpreted BEFORE testing() consumes its isCoded
    state, and testing() then silently routes to jitRunIfTest -- the hand-built
    smoke scaffold -- instead of jitRunAction. Measured on J3, 2026-07-31.  */
jbIn = 10;
jbOut = 0;
jitJ2();
print "J2 interpreted  : jbOut =" jbOut " (the oracle -- want 20)":;

print "J2 SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
